<BaseLayout title="Flat Bed Conveyors: Slider Bed vs Roller Bed Belt Guide" description="Flat bed conveyor (flat belt conveyor) guide: slider bed vs roller bed compared on load, length, and small parts, plus belt tracking, tensioning, and belting." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Flat Belt &amp; Slider Bed Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A flat belt conveyor, also called a flat bed conveyor, uses a continuous flat belt stretched between head and tail pulleys to carry products on its upper surface. The belt rides on either a smooth slider bed (sheet metal pan) or a roller bed (support rollers beneath the belt). Flat belt conveyors handle products of any size, shape, and bottom surface, including items too small, soft, or irregularly shaped for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>. They are the most versatile general-purpose conveyor type in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging</a> and material handling.</p>
<h2 id="bed-heading">Slider Bed vs. Roller Bed Conveyor</h2>
<p>A slider bed conveyor slides the belt over a flat metal pan, while a roller bed conveyor carries the belt on closely spaced rollers. Choose a slider bed for small, light, or unstable products that need continuous support and tight end transfers. Choose a roller bed for heavier loads and longer runs, where lower belt friction reduces drive power. The <a href="#comparison-heading">comparison table</a> below lists typical load, length, and speed ranges for each bed type.</p>
<h3>Slider Bed Conveyors</h3>
<p>Slider bed conveyors support the belt on a smooth metal pan (the slider bed or wear strip). The belt slides across the pan surface, driven by friction between the belt bottom and the drive pulley. The pan provides continuous support across the entire belt width, making slider beds ideal for small products that would fall between rollers and for applications requiring a flat, stable conveying surface.</p>
<p>Slider bed conveyors handle light to moderate loads: typically under 75 lbs per linear foot of belt. Belt-to-pan friction limits practical length to approximately 50 feet for standard constructions. Longer runs require more drive power to overcome the sliding friction. UHMW polyethylene strips or stainless steel pans reduce friction and extend practical length. Dorner, mk North America, and QC Industries specialize in precision slider bed conveyors for packaging applications.</p>
<h3>Roller Bed Conveyors</h3>
<p>Roller bed conveyors support the belt on closely spaced carry rollers (typically 4-6 inch spacing). The rollers spin freely as the belt moves over them, reducing friction compared to slider beds. Lower friction means longer runs (100+ feet), heavier loads (200+ lbs per linear foot), and lower power consumption. Roller bed belt conveyors handle heavier products and longer distances than slider beds.</p>
<p>Roller bed belt conveyors serve heavy packaging applications: cases, totes, and containers that are too heavy for slider beds. They also serve long transport runs between processes where the belt's continuous surface handles products that rollers cannot (irregular shapes, small items). Hytrol, Dematic, and Intelligrated offer roller bed belt conveyors for distribution and packaging applications.</p>
<h3>Knife-Edge Transfer</h3>
<p>Slider bed conveyors can be designed with a knife-edge (thin-nose) transfer at the discharge end. The drive pulley diameter is minimized to create a sharp belt edge that transfers small products with minimal gap to the next conveyor. Knife-edge transfers handle products as small as 0.5 inches. Standard roller bed conveyors cannot achieve knife-edge transfers due to the larger drive pulley required for belt wrap. Dorner and mk North America offer knife-edge transfer belt conveyors for small-product packaging lines.</p>
<h2 id="tracking-heading">Belt Tracking</h2>
<p>Belt tracking keeps the belt centered on the pulleys and bed. A mistracking belt drifts to one side, rubbing against the frame, damaging the belt edge, and potentially dumping products off the side. Proper tracking is the single most important belt conveyor maintenance task.</p>
<h3>Tracking Methods</h3>
<p><strong>Tail pulley adjustment:</strong> The primary tracking method. Screw adjusters at each end of the tail pulley shaft allow the operator to angle the tail pulley axis slightly. The belt tracks toward the tight (higher-tension) side. Small adjustments (1/8 turn of the adjustment screw) move the belt. Adjust in small increments and allow the belt to stabilize (3-5 full belt revolutions) before adjusting again.</p>
<p><strong>V-guide tracking:</strong> A V-shaped guide strip on the belt underside rides in a V-groove on the pulley or slider bed. The guide physically constrains the belt to center. V-guide tracking is common on precision slider bed conveyors (Dorner, QC Industries) where belt mistracking would disrupt product positioning for labeling, inspection, or packaging operations.</p>
<p><strong>Crowned pulleys:</strong> Crowned (barrel-shaped) pulleys naturally center the belt. The belt climbs toward the highest point of the crown. Light-duty conveyors use crowned pulleys as the primary tracking mechanism. Heavy-duty conveyors pair crowned pulleys with tail-pulley adjustment so the belt holds center under load.</p>
<p><strong>Training idlers:</strong> Roller bed conveyors use self-aligning training idlers that tilt automatically when the belt drifts, steering it back to center. Training idlers mount at strategic positions along the conveyor length and provide automatic tracking correction without manual adjustment.</p>
<h2 id="tension-heading">Belt Tensioning</h2>
<p>Proper belt tension ensures the belt grips the drive pulley without slipping while minimizing wear on bearings, belt, and pulleys. Under-tensioned belts slip on the drive pulley, causing erratic speed, heat buildup, and belt wear. Over-tensioned belts overload bearings, stretch the belt, and accelerate component wear.</p>
<h3>Screw Take-Up</h3>
<p>Screw take-ups use threaded rods at the tail pulley to slide the pulley shaft forward or backward, adjusting belt tension. The operator turns the take-up screws to increase or decrease the distance between the head and tail pulleys. Screw take-ups are the most common tensioning method on conveyor lengths under 50 feet. Adjustment is manual and periodic, check belt tension during routine maintenance.</p>
<h3>Spring and Gravity Take-Up</h3>
<p>Spring take-ups use compression springs to maintain constant belt tension automatically. As the belt stretches with use, the springs compensate by pushing the tail pulley outward. Gravity take-ups use a weighted carriage that maintains tension through gravity force. Both methods provide automatic tensioning that reduces maintenance frequency. They suit longer conveyors and applications where consistent tension matters for product handling precision.</p>
<h3>Tension Indicators</h3>
<p>Belt slip is the primary indicator of under-tension: the belt moves slower than the drive pulley, products accumulate at the infeed, and the drive pulley may generate heat and noise. Belt sag between carry rollers (roller bed) or belt camber (bowing to one side) indicates tension issues. Excessive bearing temperature indicates over-tension. A properly tensioned belt has just enough tension to prevent slipping under maximum load, no more.</p>
<h2 id="belt-types-heading">Belt Material Selection</h2>
<p><strong>PVC belting:</strong> The most common belt material for general packaging. PVC belts handle temperatures from -10 to 180°F, resist moisture and mild chemicals, and come in smooth, textured, or grip-top surfaces. Standard PVC belts suit case handling, carton transport, and general <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> applications.</p>
<p><strong>Polyurethane belting:</strong> Polyurethane belts provide higher abrasion resistance, better cut resistance, and wider chemical compatibility than PVC. They suit rough products, sharp-edged items, and environments with chemical exposure. Polyurethane costs more than PVC but lasts longer under demanding conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Silicone belting:</strong> Silicone belts handle high temperatures (up to 500°F) and FDA-compliant food contact. They serve bakery conveyors, oven discharge, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food packaging</a> where direct product contact requires food-grade material at elevated temperatures.</p>
<p><strong>Modular plastic belting:</strong> Modular belts use interlocking plastic segments instead of a continuous flat belt. Modular belts handle curves, inclines, and harsh washdown environments. Individual damaged segments replace without replacing the entire belt. Intralox, Rexnord, and Habasit supply modular belt components. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">chain conveyor guide</a> for table-top chain (a form of modular belting).</p>
<p><strong>Incline belting:</strong> Incline belt conveyors use belts with textured surfaces (rough-top, chevron, cleated) to prevent products from sliding backward on inclines. Rough-top belts handle inclines up to 25 degrees for flat-bottom products. Cleated belts handle inclines up to 45 degrees for loose products. Incline belt conveyors connect floor-level operations to elevated <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>, mezzanines, and overhead conveyors.</p>
