<h2 id="apps-heading">Belt Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Small product packaging:</strong> Slider bed belt conveyors with knife-edge transfers handle small products, bottles, pouches, cartons, blister packs, between filling, labeling, and case packing. The continuous belt surface supports products too small for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>. Dorner and mk North America specialize in precision slider bed systems for small-product packaging.</p>
<p><strong>Inspection and scanning:</strong> Belt conveyors provide a stable, flat surface for machine vision inspection, barcode scanning, and checkweighing. Products travel at consistent speed on the belt without vibration or rotation. The belt surface color (white, black, blue) contrasts with the product for vision system accuracy. Belt speed synchronizes with camera frame rates for sharp image capture.</p>
<p><strong>Incline and decline transport:</strong> Belt conveyors handle inclines that roller conveyors cannot (products slide backward on inclined rollers). Rough-top belts carry cases up 15-25 degree inclines between floor levels. Cleated belts carry loose items up steeper inclines. Incline belt conveyors connect ground-level processes to mezzanines, <a href="/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/">spiral conveyors</a>, and <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation system</a> infeeds.</p>
<p><strong>Sortation induction:</strong> Belt conveyors with servo drives create precise product spacing (gapping) for sortation system induction. The belt accelerates each product to create a programmed gap between consecutive items. This gap ensures the <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/">sort mechanism</a> has time to reset between diverts. Induction belt conveyors run at 200-400 FPM for high-speed sortation.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food processing and packaging:</a></strong> FDA-compliant belt conveyors handle direct food contact in bakery, confectionery, meat, and produce packaging. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary belt conveyors</a> use stainless steel frames, food-grade belting (polyurethane, silicone, or modular plastic), and tool-free belt release for cleaning and inspection.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Zone accumulation:</a></strong> Servo-driven zone belt conveyors provide accumulation for products that cannot ride on rollers. Each belt zone operates independently with its own drive, providing zero-pressure accumulation for irregular shapes, polybags, and flexible packaging. Belt zone conveyors serve as an alternative to <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR roller conveyors</a> when the product demands a flat surface.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a flat belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A continuous flat belt on head and tail pulleys carrying products on its upper surface. Flat bed conveyor is a common name for the same design. Slider bed (belt on a pan) for light loads and short runs. Roller bed (belt on rollers) for heavier loads and longer runs. The most versatile conveyor type. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/belt-conveyors/">belt conveyor applications</a> guide for all belt sub-types.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Slider bed vs roller bed, which do I need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Slider bed for light loads (under 75 lbs/ft), short runs (under 50 ft), small products, and knife-edge transfers. Roller bed for heavier loads (up to 200+ lbs/ft), longer runs (100+ ft), and lower power consumption. Most packaging applications use slider bed for small products and roller bed for case handling.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do I track and tension a belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Tracking: adjust tail pulley screws in small increments (1/8 turn), wait 3-5 belt revolutions between adjustments. V-guides and crowned pulleys provide automatic centering. Tensioning: screw take-ups for manual adjustment, spring/gravity take-ups for automatic. Proper tension prevents slipping without overloading bearings.</p>

</div>
</div>
